<header><p class="st-kicker">county · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">Dauphiné of Viennois</h1><p class="st-meta">1029 CE – 1349 CE (approx.) · capital Grenoble</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Alpine principality around Grenoble whose last ruler sold it to France on condition that the heir to the throne bear the title Dauphin.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Around 1029 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guigues-i-of-albon">Guigues the Old</a>, count of Albon, began gathering lands in the Viennois and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gresivaudan">Grésivaudan</a> as royal power in the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-arles">Kingdom of Arles</a> weakened. His descendants made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grenoble">Grenoble</a> their centre, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guigues-iv-of-albon">Guigues IV</a>, nicknamed '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dauphin-of-france">Dauphin</a>', gave the family its distinctive title in the 12th century.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The dauphins governed a mountain land of pastures, mines and passes towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, and later added the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brianconnais">Briançonnais</a> and Gapençais. In 1339 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humbert-ii-of-viennois">Humbert II</a> founded a university at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grenoble">Grenoble</a>. The principality had its own council and courts, and its lords balanced between <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-savoy">Savoy</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-provence">Provence</a> and the French crown.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humbert-ii-of-viennois">Humbert II</a>, childless and deep in debt, sold the Dauphiné to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-vi-of-france">Philip VI</a> under the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, completed in 1349. It went to the king's grandson Charles, and from then on the eldest son of the French king held the title <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dauphin-of-france">Dauphin</a>, while the province joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/dauphine-of-viennois.jpg" alt="Dauphiné of Viennois" width="320" height="308"><figcaption>Image: Milenioscuro · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dauphine_in_France_(1789).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1029 CE – 1349 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Grenoble</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Guigues the Old, Guigues IV of Albon, Humbert II of Viennois</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Franco-Provençal</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>France</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Dauphiné, Viennois</dd></dl></aside></div>
